window.REVIEW_DETAILS["ny_b1-1688a31a756fa966"]={"key":"ny_b1-1688a31a756fa966","group":"ny_b1","uid":"1688a31a756fa966","rank":91,"title":"sheafield duncastle renewal position","occupation":"retail_sales_supervisors","archetype":"authored_doc","subbatch":"corpus_b1","prompt":"You are the floor supervisor for Sheafield Holdings' fourteen home-goods stores, and Valeria Falconer, our regional ops manager, needs your position before she takes the renewal call with Stellan Chakraborty at Duncastle Logistics in two weeks. Duncastle's 6 a.m. Tuesday delivery has arrived after noon eleven times since March, and the knock-on effect has been missed Saturday floor sets, disrupted colleagues, and avoidable pressure on stores. Write a two-page negotiating position and concession-sequence brief for Valeria to use live on that call, and save it as `sheafield_duncastle_renewal_position.docx` in the top level of the working directory. It needs to say what we should open with, the single outcome we should protect, what we can trade and in what order, and how Valeria should respond when Stellan pushes back. Include a concise operating and commercial rationale that a senior decision maker can use without asking you to translate store-floor consequences. You will need to make the practical gap visible where the brief does not contain a complete cost history: do not present invented historical savings, charges, or service statistics as facts, and distinguish any planning assumptions or illustrative figures from known information. Keep the document focused on the renewal conversation rather than turning it into a contract, legal opinion, procurement approval, or finance case; those decisions belong to the relevant functions. Edith Lombardi, Jelena Ilechukwu, and Astrid Salcedo may read it as senior stakeholders, so make the language direct, teach the reader how to use the position under pressure, and keep the recommendation clear. Duncastle and Sheafield colleagues use “on time” in different ways, so establish what that phrase means in this brief before relying on it. End with the decision Valeria should take into the call, not a list of follow-up tasks.\n\n---\n\nWrite your finished work to the **top level of the current working directory**, using exactly these filenames:\n\n- `sheafield_duncastle_renewal_position.docx` — A two-page, live-use negotiating position and concession-sequence brief for the Duncastle Logistics renewal call. (Exactly two pages when rendered with ordinary document settings.; Usable as a live executive briefing document.; Must distinguish known facts, assumptions, and illustrative figures.; Must not become a legal opinion, contract, procurement approval, or finance case.; Must end with one clear decision rather than a follow-up list.)\n\nNothing outside those filenames is collected. A correct file written anywhere else, or under any other name, scores zero.\n","inputs":[],"gold":[{"name":"package/private/gold/sheafield_duncastle_renewal_position.docx","bytes":41863,"extension":".docx","url":"assets/3776f13e661234bdbdfd020b0163558eeffa43feec6f25a46e99f81d20a62c9e.docx","sha256":"3776f13e661234bdbdfd020b0163558eeffa43feec6f25a46e99f81d20a62c9e","preview":{"kind":"text","text":"DECISION BRIEF\nDuncastle renewal: protect the usable Tuesday delivery window\nFor Valeria Falconer | Call with Stellan Chakraborty, Duncastle Logistics | Use live on the renewal call\nOPEN WITH THIS\n“Sheafield Holdings needs a renewal that makes Tuesday stock usable for the next floor reset, not merely delivered at some point that day. Across our fourteen home-goods stores, the 6 a.m. Tuesday delivery arrived after noon eleven times since March. That has forced colleagues to abandon planned work and has disrupted Saturday floor sets. Can Duncastle commit to a reliable window that gives each affected store time to receive, replenish and prepare before its applicable POG reset?”\nPOSITION AT A GLANCE\nONE RECOMMENDATION\nRenew with Duncastle only if it commits to a reliable Tuesday delivery window that enables the relevant pre-POG-reset work. Move on lesser route-efficiency and commercial terms, conditionally; do not exchange the usable window.\nPROTECTED OUTCOME\nThe delivery window before the relevant POG reset. The result is shelves and displays prepared for the planned floor set, not a preferred clock time in isolation.\nSTOPPING BOUNDARY\nIf Duncastle will not protect that usable pre-reset window, stop conceding, restate the requirement, and do not endorse renewal on this operating position.\nSHARED DEFINITION BEFORE BARGAINING\nOn time means arrival early enough for the receiving, replenishment and floor-set preparation required before the applicable POG reset. Duncastle may reasonably use “on time” to mean physically received on the scheduled calendar day; Sheafield’s operating requirement is a usable delivery. A pallet received after the preparation window is physically received, but the merchandise is not available in time for its intended floor work.\nApply a common outcome standard, not an identical clock across every store: reset timing and receiving capacity can vary by store or reset. The agreed window must be early enough for the affected store’s pre-reset work.\nOPERATING AND COMMERCIAL RATIONALE\nKNOWN OPERATING EVIDENCE\n• Service pattern. 6 a.m. Tuesday delivery; eleven arrivals after noon since March.\n• Floor effect. Late stock disrupts Saturday floor sets and POG-reset preparation.\n• Leadership effect. Colleagues are redeployed from planned set and replenishment work; customer-facing availability and selling time can be put under pressure.\nDISCIPLINE FOR THE CALL\n• Evidence limit. The reported late-delivery history is not a complete cost ledger. Do not state historical savings, charges, losses, labour hours or service rates as facts.\n• Leverage. Use the operating consequence to explain why a usable window matters; it is not a certification of damages or contractual liability.\n• Authority. Legal/procurement interpret contract wording, remedies and enforceability. Finance validates any budget, saving or chargeback effect before commitment.\n\n\nLIVE CALL PLAYBOOK  |  Move in order. Every move is conditional.\nCONCESSION SEQUENCE: VALUE ONLY FOR A USABLE TUESDAY COMMITMENT\nMOVE\nTRADEABLE TERM\nOFFER ONLY IF DUNCASTLE...\nSHEAFIELD SEEKS / WHY IT MATTERS\n1\nConsolidated pallets\ncommits to the usable Tuesday pre-reset window, named escalation contact, and a simple weekly exception review.\nSeek: fewer handling touches / a more predictable load build for Duncastle, in exchange for a reliable arrival plan. This is first because it is operationally reversible and does not dilute the service outcome.\n2\nLater Thursday drop\nkeeps the Tuesday window intact and confirms Thursday timing will not interfere with the applicable store work.\nSeek: route flexibility or stop sequencing that may reduce Duncastle’s route pressure, in exchange for the protected Tuesday result. Thursday flexibility is separate from Tuesday usability.\n3\nSmall-carton chargeback claims / chargeback waiver\nhas first delivered the protected Tuesday commitment and offers a measurable service-recovery commitment for misses.\nSeek: reliable-window performance evidence, prompt exception notification and a recovery plan. Chargeback relief is last: it has commercial value and must not be offered before lower-cost options are tested.\nUNDER PRESSURE: Do not jump ahead. Ask for the Tuesday commitment; offer move 1 only for its stated return; test move 2 only if more route value is needed; reserve chargeback relief for move 3. If the window is refused or blurred into “same-day delivery,” stop and restate the boundary.\nLIKELY PUSHBACK AND VALERIA’S RESPONSE\nSTELLAN MAY SAY\n“Reducing stops or lowering route cost needs more flexibility.”\nVALERIA SAYS\n“We can explore consolidated pallets and a later Thursday drop because those may help your route. But route efficiency cannot substitute for stock being usable before the Tuesday POG reset. Give us a reliable delivery window, then we can trade those options in order.”\nUSEFUL CALL LANGUAGE AND BOUNDARIES\nASK FOR THE COMMITMENT\n“Can Duncastle commit to a reliable delivery window early enough for each affected store to complete receiving, replenishment and pre-reset preparation before its POG reset?”\n“If the answer is no, I cannot recommend moving our position on secondary terms.”\nDEFER WITHOUT SURRENDERING THE POSITION\n“I can describe the operating impact; I cannot validate a cost, chargeback amount or legal remedy on this call. Finance must validate financial effects, and legal/procurement must interpret any contractual position before commitment.”\nKeep service-level reliability separate from commercial remedies; neither is a substitute for the other.\nDECISION TO TAKE INTO THE CALL\nRecommend renewal only with Duncastle’s reliable Tuesday delivery window that protects the applicable pre-POG-reset work; trade consolidated pallets, a later Thursday drop, then small-carton chargeback relief only as conditional exchanges, and do not concede the protected window.","truncated":false,"note":"DOCX 正文与表格单元格文字提取；不还原分页、字体与版式。","embedded_image_count":0,"embedded_images":[]}}],"rubric":[{"rubric_item_id":"R01","check":"file_introspection","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The submitted file is a readable DOCX named sheafield_duncastle_renewal_position.docx.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Filename and successful DOCX opening.","grader_hint":"Inspect the top-level submission and open the document."},{"rubric_item_id":"R02","check":"geometry","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The brief renders to no more than two pages and is not effectively empty or unreadably compressed.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Rendered page count and legibility.","grader_hint":"Render the DOCX using standard office tooling and inspect both pages."},{"rubric_item_id":"R03","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The brief identifies Sheafield Holdings as the customer and Duncastle Logistics as the logistics provider.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Named parties in the document.","grader_hint":"Search the rendered text."},{"rubric_item_id":"R04","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The brief identifies Valeria Falconer as the person using the position in the renewal call.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"User and call context.","grader_hint":"Search for Valeria Falconer and confirm the live-call role."},{"rubric_item_id":"R05","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The brief accurately carries through the known operating facts: fourteen stores, a 6 a.m. Tuesday delivery, eleven arrivals after noon since March, and disruption to Saturday floor sets.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Fact pattern in the situation section.","grader_hint":"Verify each supplied fact and that no supplied fact is materially altered."},{"rubric_item_id":"R06","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The opening gives an executive a concise statement of the business problem rather than a generic introduction.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Opening paragraph or call objective.","grader_hint":"Judge whether the first reading makes the operational consequence immediately clear."},{"rubric_item_id":"R07","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The brief explicitly defines “on time” as arrival early enough for the relevant pre-reset store work, rather than merely arrival on the delivery day.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Definition of the disputed term.","grader_hint":"Look for a plain-language operational definition tied to the floor-set deadline."},{"rubric_item_id":"R08","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The definition distinguishes the carrier's possible interpretation of an on-day delivery from Sheafield's usable-delivery requirement.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Comparison of interpretations.","grader_hint":"Confirm that the document prevents the two parties from claiming success under different clocks."},{"rubric_item_id":"R09","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The brief states that the delivery window before the relevant POG reset is the protected outcome.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Objective or non-negotiable section.","grader_hint":"Search for the protected service requirement and its connection to the reset."},{"rubric_item_id":"R10","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The protected outcome is expressed as an operational result, not just a preferred clock time.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Outcome wording.","grader_hint":"Credit language that explains why timing matters to store execution."},{"rubric_item_id":"R11","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The brief gives exactly one primary recommendation for the renewal stance.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Recommendation statement.","grader_hint":"Do not credit a menu of equal alternatives."},{"rubric_item_id":"R12","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The recommendation protects the pre-reset delivery requirement while allowing movement on lesser terms.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Recommendation and trade-off logic.","grader_hint":"Check that the central call is neither an unconditional renewal nor an all-or-nothing rejection."},{"rubric_item_id":"R13","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The recommendation is justified by the effect of late deliveries on floor-set execution and store operations.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Rationale paragraph.","grader_hint":"The rationale should connect service failure to consequences a senior reader can evaluate."},{"rubric_item_id":"R14","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The brief identifies consolidated pallets as a tradeable term.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Concession table or sequence.","grader_hint":"Search for consolidated pallets and confirm they are not labelled non-negotiable."},{"rubric_item_id":"R15","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The brief identifies a later Thursday drop as a tradeable term.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Concession table or sequence.","grader_hint":"Search for Thursday and confirm the term is treated as negotiable."},{"rubric_item_id":"R16","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The brief identifies the small-carton chargeback claims or chargeback waiver as a tradeable term.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Commercial trade-off section.","grader_hint":"Confirm the chargeback issue is explicitly addressed."},{"rubric_item_id":"R17","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The concession sequence preserves the delivery-window requirement as the last or protected item rather than offering it early.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Ordered concession sequence.","grader_hint":"Inspect the order and the stated boundary around the service window."},{"rubric_item_id":"R18","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The sequence does not give away the chargeback waiver before other available value has been tested.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Ordered concessions.","grader_hint":"Check that chargeback relief is not the first concession."},{"rubric_item_id":"R19","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Each concession is conditional on a reciprocal improvement or commitment from Duncastle.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Wording beside each concession.","grader_hint":"Credit explicit if/then exchanges, not unilateral giveaways."},{"rubric_item_id":"R20","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The sequence shows why the first concession is lower-cost or more operationally reversible than later concessions.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Sequence rationale.","grader_hint":"Look for an intelligible escalation of value."},{"rubric_item_id":"R21","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The brief sets a clear stopping boundary if Duncastle will not protect the required delivery window.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Walk-away or escalation boundary.","grader_hint":"Credit a concrete boundary, not vague language such as 'seek a better deal'."},{"rubric_item_id":"R22","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The brief gives Valeria a usable opening statement or opening position for the call.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Call script or opening box.","grader_hint":"Judge whether it could be read aloud without improvising the core ask."},{"rubric_item_id":"R23","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The opening states the service problem and the desired outcome before discussing concessions.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Opening sequence.","grader_hint":"Confirm that bargaining over secondary terms does not precede the core service ask."},{"rubric_item_id":"R24","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The brief includes a concise response to a likely Duncastle counterargument about reducing stops or route cost.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Counter-response language.","grader_hint":"Look for a response that acknowledges the carrier's interest without surrendering the protected outcome."},{"rubric_item_id":"R25","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The counter-response preserves the distinction between route-efficiency concessions and the usable delivery window.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Counter-response logic.","grader_hint":"Credit only if the carrier's cost concern is answered without treating late arrival as equivalent service."},{"rubric_item_id":"R26","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The brief explains that late deliveries disrupt Saturday floor sets or equivalent POG-reset preparation.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Operational impact statement.","grader_hint":"Search for floor sets, POG reset, or a precise equivalent."},{"rubric_item_id":"R27","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The brief identifies at least two distinct floor consequences beyond lateness itself, such as colleague redeployment, customer availability, replenishment, or lost selling time.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Impact analysis.","grader_hint":"Require two materially different consequences."},{"rubric_item_id":"R28","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The operational consequences are described in terms useful to a senior decision maker rather than retail jargon alone.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Impact prose.","grader_hint":"Judge clarity for Edith Lombardi, Jelena Ilechukwu, or Astrid Salcedo as non-specialist stakeholders."},{"rubric_item_id":"R29","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document separates facts supplied in the brief from estimates, assumptions, or illustrative planning figures.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Labels, notes, or assumptions box.","grader_hint":"Check whether a reader can tell what is known versus modelled."},{"rubric_item_id":"R30","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document does not present an invented historical cost, charge, saving, revenue loss, or service percentage as an established Sheafield fact.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"All quantitative claims.","grader_hint":"Treat unsupported figures as acceptable only when explicitly marked illustrative or assumed."},{"rubric_item_id":"R31","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Where figures are used, the brief explains what decision or trade-off each figure is intended to inform.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Quantitative rationale.","grader_hint":"Do not credit decorative arithmetic."},{"rubric_item_id":"R32","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Any illustrative financial comparison is internally consistent across the recommendation, concession sequence, and closing decision.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Repeated figures and labels.","grader_hint":"Recalculate simple totals and compare repeated references."},{"rubric_item_id":"R33","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The brief acknowledges that the current evidence is limited to the reported late-delivery history rather than a complete cost ledger.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Evidence limitation statement.","grader_hint":"Look for a direct limitation, not merely an absence of numbers."},{"rubric_item_id":"R34","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The brief recommends using operational impact as negotiation leverage without claiming that the supervisor has authority to certify contractual damages.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Evidence and authority language.","grader_hint":"Confirm the distinction between explaining impact and legally establishing liability."},{"rubric_item_id":"R35","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The brief marks legal or contractual interpretation as belonging to the appropriate legal or procurement function.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Boundary statement.","grader_hint":"Search for a clear handoff or authority boundary."},{"rubric_item_id":"R36","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document does not claim that the supervisor can amend, waive, or enforce contract terms unilaterally.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Authority claims.","grader_hint":"Check for overreach in wording such as 'we will waive' or 'Duncastle is liable'."},{"rubric_item_id":"R37","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The brief marks finance authority as responsible for validating any budget, savings, or chargeback effect before commitment.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Boundary or approval note.","grader_hint":"Credit a clear finance boundary even if no financial model is included."},{"rubric_item_id":"R38","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The boundary language remains useful to Valeria and does not simply disclaim responsibility for the entire negotiation.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Boundary section in context.","grader_hint":"Judge whether the supervisor still makes a practical operating recommendation."},{"rubric_item_id":"R39","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The brief distinguishes the service-level ask from the commercial remedies or chargeback discussion.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Negotiation architecture.","grader_hint":"Ensure the remedies are not treated as substitutes for reliable delivery."},{"rubric_item_id":"R40","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The concession sequence is visually scannable during a live call.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Table, numbered sequence, or equivalent navigation.","grader_hint":"Judge whether the user can find the next move within seconds."},{"rubric_item_id":"R41","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Every listed tradeable term has a stated reason it might matter to Duncastle or a route-efficiency rationale.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Trade-off descriptions.","grader_hint":"Require a carrier-side rationale for each of the three tradeables."},{"rubric_item_id":"R42","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The brief states what Sheafield should seek in exchange for consolidated pallets.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Concession exchange.","grader_hint":"Look for a reciprocal service or commitment."},{"rubric_item_id":"R43","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The brief states what Sheafield should seek in exchange for accepting a later Thursday drop.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Concession exchange.","grader_hint":"Confirm that Thursday flexibility does not dilute the protected Tuesday requirement."},{"rubric_item_id":"R44","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The brief states what Sheafield should seek before reducing or waiving small-carton chargeback claims.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Chargeback exchange.","grader_hint":"Require a reciprocal improvement or measurable commitment."},{"rubric_item_id":"R45","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The sequence tells Valeria when to stop conceding and restate the protected requirement.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Escalation language.","grader_hint":"Look for a practical stopping cue."},{"rubric_item_id":"R46","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The brief includes language Valeria can use to ask Duncastle to commit to a reliable delivery window.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Suggested call language.","grader_hint":"Credit a usable sentence or tightly written prompt."},{"rubric_item_id":"R47","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The brief includes language Valeria can use to defer unsupported cost or legal claims to the responsible function.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Suggested call language.","grader_hint":"Look for a practical deferral phrase rather than a disclaimer only."},{"rubric_item_id":"R48","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The tone is firm, commercially credible, and non-accusatory toward Stellan Chakraborty.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Whole-document tone.","grader_hint":"Judge suitability for a renewal conversation with a carrier account manager."},{"rubric_item_id":"R49","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The brief avoids treating all fourteen stores as having identical delivery needs when the operational rationale may vary by store or reset.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Scope and operating language.","grader_hint":"Credit an acknowledgement of store-level variation or a defensible reason to use a common standard."},{"rubric_item_id":"R50","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The brief gives enough context for a senior reader who was not present for the eleven late deliveries to understand the decision.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Context and rationale.","grader_hint":"Judge standalone comprehensibility."},{"rubric_item_id":"R51","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The brief distinguishes a delivery being physically received from merchandise being available in time for the intended floor work.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Operational definition and impact logic.","grader_hint":"This is central to resolving the disputed meaning of on time."},{"rubric_item_id":"R52","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The document teaches Valeria how to apply the sequence under pressure rather than merely listing negotiation principles.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Call-use instructions and conditional language.","grader_hint":"Judge practical instructional value for a live call."},{"rubric_item_id":"R53","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The close repeats the same single recommendation and protected outcome established earlier.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Final decision statement compared with opening recommendation.","grader_hint":"Check for internal agreement and no late change of position."},{"rubric_item_id":"R54","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document ends on the decision Valeria should carry into the call and does not end with a list of next steps or open questions.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Final paragraph or callout.","grader_hint":"Inspect the final substantive text."},{"rubric_item_id":"R55","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The complete brief is practical to navigate, sufficiently concise for a two-page live-use document, and usable without asking its author to interpret the core position.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Whole rendered artifact.","grader_hint":"Professional reviewer should assess hierarchy, scanability, clarity, and independent usability together."}],"spec":{"professional_context":"A regional retail operations supervisor is preparing a concise live-use negotiation brief for an executive who will renew a logistics contract. The work relies on retail-floor operating knowledge and defensible assumptions rather than supplied records.","difficulty_sources":["material ambiguity about the meaning of on-time delivery","a negotiation brief must distinguish store-operations evidence from procurement, legal, and finance authority","the practitioner must reconcile a non-negotiable service need with several tradeable commercial terms","the brief must make one recommendation without supplied cost data"],"authoring_plan":{"purpose":"Construct a two-page executive negotiation brief that teaches Valeria how to use one defensible operating position during the Duncastle renewal call.","source_constraints":{"known_facts":["Sheafield Holdings operates fourteen home-goods stores.","Duncastle Logistics has a 6 a.m. Tuesday delivery arrangement.","The delivery has arrived after noon eleven times since March.","The lateness has disrupted Saturday floor sets.","The renewal conversation is two weeks away.","Valeria Falconer will conduct the call with Stellan Chakraborty."],"must_not_invent_as_facts":["Historical delivery cost totals","Actual chargeback amounts","Actual sales losses","Actual labour hours","Actual contractual service-level wording","Actual legal remedies","Actual Duncastle capacity or route economics"]},"professional_judgment_requirements":{"core_position":"Protect the delivery window that makes the pre-POG-reset work possible; treat consolidated pallets, a later Thursday drop, and small-carton chargeback relief as negotiable value.","negotiation_logic":["Open with the operational service problem and the protected outcome.","Seek a reciprocal commitment for every concession.","Sequence lower-impact or more reversible concessions before higher-value concessions.","Do not offer chargeback relief first.","Do not trade away the protected delivery window merely to obtain route-cost relief.","Include a stopping boundary if the carrier will not address the usable delivery requirement.","Anticipate a carrier counterargument about reducing stops or route cost and answer it without equating route efficiency with usable store service."],"term_definition":"Define on time as arrival early enough for the required store receiving, replenishment, and floor-set preparation before the applicable POG reset; distinguish this from merely arriving on the scheduled calendar day.","functional_boundary":["The supervisor may explain operational impact and recommend negotiation priorities.","Legal or procurement must interpret contract language, remedies, and enforceability.","Finance must validate budget, savings, chargeback, or damage calculations before commitment.","The brief must not assert liability, amend terms, or authorize a waiver."],"evidence_handling":["Use the eleven late deliveries and operational consequences as known facts.","If an illustrative cost or threshold is useful, label it clearly as an assumption, planning figure, or example.","Keep any illustrative arithmetic internally consistent wherever repeated.","Acknowledge that a complete cost history was not supplied."],"instructional_features":["Include a read-aloud opening or opening position.","Use a compact ordered concession sequence with the condition for moving to each next concession.","Include concise language for responding to a stop-reduction or route-cost counter.","End with one decision, not a follow-up plan."]},"gold_construction":"Create a polished DOCX with an executive summary, a clear definition of on time, an operational-impact rationale, a visually scannable ordered concession sequence, a short counter-response script, authority boundaries, and a final single decision. Keep it to two ordinary rendered pages. Use no external sources and no hidden data."},"design_constraints":{"artifact_scope":"single_artifact","structure":"strict_schema"},"reference_files":[],"deliverables":[{"deliverable_id":"negotiating_position","filename":"sheafield_duncastle_renewal_position.docx","media_type":"application/vnd.openxmlformats-officedocument.wordprocessingml.document","description":"A two-page, live-use negotiating position and concession-sequence brief for the Duncastle Logistics renewal call.","required":true,"constraints":["Exactly two pages when rendered with ordinary document settings.","Usable as a live executive briefing document.","Must distinguish known facts, assumptions, and illustrative figures.","Must not become a legal opinion, contract, procurement approval, or finance case.","Must end with one clear decision rather than a follow-up list."],"rendering_required":true}],"work_activity":"plan_work","work_product":"operating_plan","internet_policy":"forbidden","evidence_policy":"closed_world"},"source_metadata":{"prompt_version":"authored_doc@v4","occupation":"retail_sales_supervisors","sector":"retail_trade","task_archetype":"authored_doc","estimated_professional_hours":2.5,"gold_policy_self_report_not_verified":{"author_type":"domain_expert","reviewer_policy":"Have an independent retail-operations reviewer and a negotiation-brief reviewer inspect the rendered DOCX for factual discipline, professional completeness, usability, and internal consistency. Use simple automated checks for filename, opening, page count, and text presence, then resolve qualitative disagreements conservatively.","minimum_reviewers":2,"known_limitations":["No supplied cost ledger exists, so quantitative analysis can only be illustrative and must be labelled.","Professional reviewers may differ on the precise ordering of the three tradeable concessions; the rubric requires defensible conditional sequencing and protection of the delivery window rather than one arbitrary numeric order beyond the explicit chargeback-first failure.","The brief is judged as an operating negotiation aid, not as a legally binding contract position."],"reference_solution_passed_rubric":true}},"validation":{"label":"成品包存在","detail":"按公开题面存在纳入抽样；不视为内容正确性验收。","formal":false,"release_ready_recorded":true,"checks":[{"check":"package_integrity","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-20T18:53:47+00:00"},{"check":"gold_isolation","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-20T18:53:47+00:00"},{"check":"artifact_format","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-20T18:53:47+00:00"},{"check":"rendering","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-20T18:53:47+00:00"},{"check":"instruction_rubric_overlap","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":false,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-20T18:53:47+00:00"}]},"source_path":"/inspire/hdd2/project/special-project-1/public/ynan/gdpval_data/corpus_b1/runs/1688a31a756fa966","declared_deliverables":["sheafield_duncastle_renewal_position.docx"]};
